We went back to Burness Paull for further clarity on their advice note on comparative advertising. 

I asked them the following question: 

*_“I have one final question in relation to your advice on Bolts basis for comparison. You have mentioned that competitors may argue that the duration and mileage might vary between companies, which means earnings may differ. We would like to know if this matters? Given that we are not talking about a particular route, the point around commission charge on any trip for £28 (the route is irrelevant) . The duration and mileage would be different due to external factors (traffic and drivers are free to take whatever route they want). All drivers are obliged to follow the meter, so the components would not change”_*

[~accountid:5d8df399c7d4e30dc2836580] please see Paulines response below in {color:#ff991f}orange{color}: 



{color:#ff991f}“What you have struck upon is precisely the argument that I would make if a competitor or the ASA criticised the advert.  It is the potential for ambiguity that leaves the advert open to criticism.  The issue (and my concern) is that the advert states that you can earn more when driving with Bolt.  If a £28 standard fare typically takes less time with another company versus Bolt because of the rate at which the meter is set, then arguably an hour on the road for Bolt would not result in earning more than an hour on the road with a company whose meter runs higher (charging customers more for a shorter journey).{color}

{color:#ff991f} I am conscious that my awareness of how charging works for taxi companies is limited.  If the meter is set at the same charge-out rate for all companies per mile or per hour, then I would have less of a concern.  I suspect this is not the case, or at least, it is not as simple as that.{color}

{color:#ff991f} Would it be helpful to discuss this on a call to help you reach a decision?  I could speak this afternoon or tomorrow if that would be helpful”{color}

